meta {
  name: Review Course Switch Request
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/college-admin/course-switch-requests/{{courseSwitchRequestId}}/review
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{staffToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "decision": "approve",
    "remarks": "Approved — target course has open seats and student meets eligibility."
  }
}

docs {
  College admin approves or rejects a pending course switch request.

  Body:
  - decision: "approve" | "reject"
  - remarks: optional, but REQUIRED when decision is "reject"

  On approve, inside one transaction:
  - A new ApplicationCourse row is created (or an existing withdrawn one
    reactivated) for the target course, under the student's ORIGINAL
    Application, status set straight to "enrolled" (the assessment/
    interview/token-payment pipeline is skipped — this is an
    administrative transfer, not a fresh admission)
  - A new Enrollment is created from it (new enrollment number,
    status: "active")
  - The OLD Enrollment.status -> "course_switched" (a documented terminal
    status)
  - The OLD ApplicationCourse.status -> "deferred" (the student's seat on
    that course is deferred, not withdrawn/dropped — they're still
    enrolled overall, just on the new course now)
  - CourseSwitchRequest.status -> "approved", newEnrollmentId set

  Every Student Hub read (BeaconU Card, commute/hostel eligibility,
  finance) automatically reflects the new course afterward — they all
  resolve off "whichever Enrollment is currently active" for the student,
  and there's exactly one (the new one) once this completes.

  Known limitations (by design, not bugs):
  - No seat-capacity check against the target course — the schema has no
    quota/seat reference on CourseSwitchRequest, so this is a fully manual
    admin judgment call, not system-enforced.
  - No automatic fee reconciliation — already-paid fees for the old course
    aren't touched. Handle any refund/adjustment per college policy and
    note it in remarks.

  409 ConflictError if the request isn't "pending" anymore, or if the
  student's original enrollment is no longer active, or if the target
  course is no longer offered under that admission cycle. 404 if the
  request doesn't belong to this college.
}
